<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oseberg ship</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">834</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oseberg ship is a richly carved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> ship found in a burial mound at Oseberg near Tønsberg in Vestfold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, and excavated in 1904. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> were felled in 820, and the burial took place in 834. It held two women with sledges, a wagon, textiles and animals, and its carvings are the finest surviving early Viking art. It is displayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oslo">Oslo</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oseberg-ship.jpg" alt="Oseberg ship" width="320" height="230" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Petter Ulleland · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Osebergskipet_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
